statement:
Each of the strings `s` and `t` writes down a non-negative rational number in one of three forms:

- `I`: only an integer part, such as `7` or `120`;
- `I.N`: an integer part, a dot and a non-repeating part of decimals, which may be empty (`3.` is allowed);
- `I.N(R)`: as above, followed by a block `R` in parentheses whose digits repeat after `N` forever; `N` may be empty here too.

Return `true` when `s` and `t` stand for exactly the same mathematical value and `false` otherwise. Values are compared exactly, so an expansion whose digits end in an endless run of 9s equals the number you get by rounding it up.

validator:
function validate([s, t]) {
  const shape = /^(0|[1-9]\d{0,3})(\.\d{0,4}(\(\d{1,4}\))?)?$/;
  for (const [name, text] of [['s', s], ['t', t]]) {
    if (typeof text !== 'string' || !shape.test(text)) return `${name} must look like I, I.N or I.N(R): I has 1 to 4 digits without leading zeros, N 0 to 4 digits, R 1 to 4 digits.`;
  }
  return null;
}

